
The sources here should simply compile by typing make. My current env is a 
Linux 2.2.12 machine and using egcs 2.95.2. (At some point the project will
be autoconf'd ...) The only problems I can forsee in different environments 
would be include files or library differences (most of the code is very 
platform agnostic ...). I also will probably be migrating this to an OpenBSD
based machine in the near future, so platform wierdities will be addressed 
then.


After successfully compiling the daemon "jukend" can be satrted. This daemon 
will manage communications to and from the player, and processes traffic for 
the individual tools. To start the daemon execute:

    $ ./jukend
      disc#: 26 track: 1 (of23)
              mode: track
              userfiles: 00
              unknown: 00
      ready: 0x00
      state: stopped
      disc#: 26

The daemon will communicate to the player on serial port /dev/ttys0 by default
(an alternate serial port can be specified on the command line). I usually run
the daemon in a term of its own so I can watch its ouitput independently of the
tools.

To initiate a session with the player, the handshake tool should be executed as:
    $ ./handshake
      connected to CD-425M
    $

At this point the connection to the player is active, and the jukend daemon
will print out messages received from the player. So, if you change a disc in the player, the following string would be printed:


Also, after the handshake has executed, other tools can be executed. For example
the disc titles can be listed by:
    $ ./querydisctitles
      slot:  26 unknown:00 userfiles: 00 unknown: 00 genre:Rock Music unknown:00 title:Tom Petty/Full Moon Fever
      slot:  28 unknown:00 userfiles: 00 unknown: 00 genre:Rock Music unknown:00 title:Police/Reggatta De Blanc
      slot:  49 unknown:00 userfiles: 00 unknown: 00 genre:Country unknown:00 title:Creedence/Chronicle V2
      slot:  74 unknown:00 userfiles: 00 unknown: 00 genre:Rhythm & Blues unknown:00 title:Eric Clapton: Unplugged

    $
